Our research investigates asymptotic properties of $\hat{
arrow}$, defined as the initial segment comprising $o(n)$ elements of uniformly selected parking functions of length $n$. We demonstrate that with high probability, all entries within $\hat{
arrow}$ satisfy $f_i=	heta+O(\log n)$, where $	heta$ represents an arbitrary constant that grows slower than $n$ (i.e., $	heta=\omega(1)$, $	heta\ll n$). Additionally, we determine the limit distribution that this segment adheres to as $n$ approaches infinity, specifically identifying it as $\Lambda_\lambda$ when $	heta=\lambda\cdot n$. In contrast to previous work by B'ela'nch et al. (2022)—which examined the case where $	heta$ remains fixed while $n$ converges—our model allows for B'ela'nch to differ in limiting distributions when parameters scale differently. When $	heta$ is fixed and $n$ tends to infinity, our study shows that with high probability all components of the segment $\hat{
